cense":"CC0"},"editorial":{"note":"Carlo Bugatti's sons achieved illustrious careers of their own. Rembrandt Bugatti (1884-1916) became a celebrated sculptor, and Ettore Bugatti (1881-1947) manufactured luxurious automobiles and racing cars.","noteSource":"source","room":"interiors","tags":["19th-century","france","furniture","furniture-and-woodwork","late-19th-century"],"tone":"sky","quality":1.29},"media":{"kind":"image","url":"https://openaccess-cdn.clevelandart.org/1984.177/1984.177_web.jpg","fallback":null,"poster":null,"start":null,"end":null,"duration":null}},"2351":{"n":2351,"cl":39,"id":"cma-143175","type":"image","cat":"visual","title":"Clock","creator":"André-Charles Boulle","date":"c. 1695","source":{"institution":"Cleveland Museum of Art, Open Access","work":null,"url":"https://clevelandart.org/art/1967.153"},"rights":{"license":"CC0"},"editorial":{"note":"The Latin phrase inscribed on this clock, Dirigit atque movet, can be translated as \"time constantly moves on.\"","noteSource":"source","room":"interiors","tags":["17th-century","france","furniture","furniture-and-woodwork","late-17th-century","gilt-bronze"],"tone":"paper","quality":1.29},"media":{"kind":"image","url":"https://openaccess-cdn.clevelandart.org/1967.153/1967.153_web.jpg","fallback":null,"poster":null,"start":null,"end":null,"duration":null}},"2362":{"n":2362,"cl":39,"id":"cma-155652","type":"image","cat":"visual","title":"Armchair","creator":"Louis Comfort Tiffany","date":"c. 1891–93","source":{"institution":"Cleveland Museum of Art, Open Access","work":null,"url":"https://clevelandart.org/art/1990.91"},"rights":{"license":"CC0"},"editorial":{"note":"Embellished with elaborate carved flowers and inlaid wooden mosaics, this armchair repeats many of the motifs found in Tiffany’s lamps.","noteSource":"source","room":"interiors","tags":["19th-century","furniture","furniture-and-woodwork","america","new-york"],"tone":"paper","quality":1.29},"media":{"kind":"image","url":"https://openaccess-cdn.clevelandart.org/1990.91/1990.91_web.jpg","fallback":null,"poster":null,"start":null,"end":null,"duration":null}},"2443":{"n":2443,"cl":39,"id":"cma-148803","type":"image","cat":"visual","title":"Cabinet","creator":"Louis Majorelle","date":"c. 1910","source":{"institution":"Cleveland Museum of Art, Open Access","work":null,"url":"https://clevelandart.org/art/1976.53"},"rights":{"license":"CC0"},"editorial":{"note":"Let your eye trace the sinuous lines and leafy forms of this chest. Its design emphasizes the beauty of the wood, celebrating nature. Louis Majorelle designed it in the Art Nouveau style.","noteSource":"source","room":"interiors","tags":["20th-century","france","furniture","furniture-and-woodwork","early-20th-century"],"tone":"mauve","quality":1.29},"media":{"kind":"image","url":"https://openaccess-cdn.clevelandart.org/1976.53/1976.53_web.jpg","fallback":null,"poster":null,"start":null,"end":null,"duration":null}},"2597":{"n":2597,"cl":39,"id":"cma-119014","type":"image","cat":"visual","title":"Mirror Case with a Couple Playing Chess","creator":"Unknown maker","date":"1325–50","source":{"institution":"Cleveland Museum of Art, Open Access","work":null,"url":"https://clevelandart.org/art/1940.1200"},"rights":{"license":"CC0"},"editorial":{"note":"Chess was a wildly popular pastime among the nobility, and was also an important element in medieval romantic literature. Who do you think is winning this game?","noteSource":"source","room":"interiors","tags":["14th-century","paris","france","med-gothic","ivory"],"tone":"mauve","quality":1.25},"media":{"kind":"image","url":"https://openaccess-cdn.clevelandart.org/1940.1200/1940.1200_web.jpg","fallback":null,"poster":null,"start":null,"end":null,"duration":null}},"2834":{"n":2834,"cl":39,"id":"cma-150188","type":"image","cat":"visual","title":"Mosque Lamp","creator":"Unknown maker","date":"late 1800s","source":{"institution":"Cleveland Museum of Art, Open Access","work":null,"url":"https://clevelandart.org/art/1981.10"},"rights":{"license":"CC0"},"editorial":{"note":"This colorful lamp would have been suspended by its six handles. When lowered to refill its oil, it could stand securely on its splayed foot.","noteSource":"source","room":"interiors","tags":["19th-century","france","glass","islamic-art"],"tone":"paper","quality":1.25},"media":{"kind":"image","url":"https://openaccess-cdn.clevelandart.org/1981.10/1981.10_web.jpg","fallback":null,"poster":null,"start":null,"end":null,"duration":null}}}